                    <note type="editorial"> See <title>DNB</title>.
<p>'Godwin was also regularly invited to the dinner table 
of Lord Lauderdale, one of the leaders of the small band of Whigs whose  persistent opposition in Parliament helped to soften the repressive policies of Pitt’s
 government.  There he met most of the leading Whig politicians, Lord Derby, Sir Philip Francis, Adair, Courtenay, North, and William Smith.’ (St Clair, p. 94)</p><p>See the note from T Moore 24 January 1795 saying that Lord lauderdale has expressed a desire to become acquainted with Godwin and invites him to dine 
on Wednesday - which he does.   There is also an undated note to Marshall who is to wait on Lauderdale's answer before seeing Sharp.
</p></note>
                    <bibl type="printedbook">St Clair</bibl>
